The automatic doors of the terminal hissed open, releasing a blast of humid air that felt heavy against his skin. Elias moved through the crowd in a daze, his movements sluggish, his mind still vibrating with the low-frequency hum of the jet engines. He felt dehydrated, his throat feeling like it had been lined with sandpaper after ten hours of recycled cabin air and lukewarm coffee.

He spotted the vending machine in the quiet corridor leading to baggage claim—a glowing monolith of neon lights and frosted glass. 

As he approached, the condensation on the glass seemed to call to him. He didn't even look at the labels;
